    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Normalising an example",
     "md": "Unnormalised: Orders(OrderID, CustomerName, CustomerTown, ProductName, Price, Qty). Customer details depend on the customer, product details on the product. Split into Customer(CID, CName, Town), Product(PID, PName, Price) and Orders(OID, CID, PID, Qty) with foreign keys: no duplicated customer or product data and each fact is stored once."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "SQL joins",
     "md": "SELECT CName, PName, Qty \\* Price FROM Orders JOIN Customer ON Orders.CID = Customer.CID JOIN Product ON Orders.PID = Product.PID; Add GROUP BY CName with SUM(...) for totals per customer. Aggregates ignore NULL."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Storing a calculated value (Qty x Price) that can be derived.\n- A join without the ON condition multiplies rows (every combination).\n- 'Repeating a customer name' is a sign that data is not normalised.\n- Using a name instead of an ID as a foreign key."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1 - Total per order",
     "statement": "Product prices: 10 Rice 600, 11 Oil 1500, 12 Sugar 800. Order 100 is customer 1, product 10, quantity 3. Compute the line total.",
     "steps": [
      "Join Orders to Product on PID: product 10 is Rice at 600.",
      "Line total = Qty x Price = 3 x 600 = 1800 FCFA."
     ],
     "answer": "1800 FCFA"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2 - Total per customer",
     "statement": "Customer 1 (Ada) has orders 100 (Rice, 3) and 101 (Oil, 1). What does SUM(Qty\\*Price) GROUP BY CName give for Ada?",
     "steps": [
      "Order 100: 3 x 600 = 1800. Order 101: 1 x 1500 = 1500.",
      "Total = 3300."
     ],
     "answer": "3300 FCFA"
    }
   ],
